From 02a26849865118535e5e161f3f8d56bc0c21d164 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hoangvu12 <hggaming91@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 11:46:29 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] An agent's message to a busy chat waits for the command it
 runs

An agent's send_message (mode "auto") to a working chat was a steer read
at once. On Claude, with a tool call open, that stopped the turn in place
first, and the CLI told the model "the user doesn't want to proceed with
this tool use ... STOP and wait for the user". Workers read the killed
gate, type check or screenshot run as "declined at the permission
prompt", and halted for nobody.

- Steer commands carry `at_once` (default true, so older peers' steers
  and the person's read at once as before). send_message's "auto" sends
  `at_once: false`: the steer goes by the mailbox, the running command
  finishes, and the message lands at the turn's next step (Claude writes
  it with priority "next"). An explicit mode "steer" is still read at
  once; the tool's description and docs/mcp.md say to use it, or
  interrupt_chat, only when the message can't wait.
- A steer that does stop a command now carries a Zeron note under it, for
  the agent only: "your running command `npm run gate` was stopped so you
  could read the message above at once. Nobody declined it; run it again
  if it's still needed." The transcript keeps the message as sent.
- The run publishes what each open call is (a command, an MCP tool) next
  to their count, for that note.

Tests: steer_now (an agent's send during a command stops nothing and lands
after it; an at-once steer's note), the MCP send's `atOnce`, the payload's
default, and in the Claude harness a message sent while a command runs is
written `next`, with no interrupt before it.
